WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.639 --> 00:00:14.490
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again. Pour yourself a cold glass of milk, cuz today we’re talking

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:19.800
about Jordan Peele’s social thriller Get Out. While many films explore race in America,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.730
what makes Get Out so unique is that it doesn’t go after the usual targets but instead takes

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:30.420
aim at an unlikely mark: white liberals. Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on Get Out.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:36.360
And, of course, spoilers ahead. But first, a quick recap: Get out follows Chris Washington as

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:40.150
he meets his white girlfriend’s parents for the first time. While the first half of

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:44.230
the film explores white liberals awkwardly attempting social interaction with a black

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:49.060
man; the second half of the film takes a pretty horrific turn. After we find out that the

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:54.240
party hosted by Rose’s parents is, in fact, a slave auction of sorts, we learn an even

00:00:54.260 --> 00:01:00.260
more terrifying truth: An old white man wants his brain implanted inside of Chris’ body.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:06.560
"You have been chosen because of the physical advantages you enjoyed your entire lifetime.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:14.820
With your natural gifts and our determination, we could both be part of something greater. Something perfect."

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:20.100
Writer/director Jordan Peele said the story came to him after reflecting on how many Americans

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:26.340
thought the Obama presidency signified a ‘post-racial’ era. The film argues that so-called post-racial

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.180
liberalism is something more terrifying than most would imagine.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.590
Throughout the film, Chris is confronted with “totally not racist” white people who

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:40.259
have ‘moved beyond’ racism. It explores how this disavowal seems to reinforce a relationship

00:01:40.259 --> 00:01:45.920
with black people that is, at its best, suspicious and at its worst... well, we’ll get there.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.260
But the first act shows us how the attempts of people to show Chris just how ‘not racist’

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.260
they are only serves to further alienate him.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:57.540
"So how long has this been going on? This... thang."

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.060
The Armitage family seems unnaturally eager

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.840
to bring up “black people stuff” around Chris- as if this proves that they’re not racist.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:11.520
"By the way, I woulda voted for Obama for a third term if I could. Best president in my lifetime, hands down."

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.060
This tendency to over-perform the “acceptance” of all things black only

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:21.231
further cements the difference between them, and makes Chris extremely uncomfortable.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.320
Chris just wants to to get through the weekend and take some pictures, but everyone around him

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.200
can’t help reminding him that he’s black, thus only making him feel more “different”.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.160
"Gordon was a professional golfer for years!"

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:33.860
"Oh, you kidding?"

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:40.100
Well I can't quite swing the hips like I used to though, but I do know Tiger."

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.060
This also leads to a situation where any actual racism can be excused as long as one can show

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:50.060
how “down” they are with black culture and anti-racist ideas.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:57.520
"Cmon, I get it. White family, black servants, it's a total cliche."

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.600
"I wasn't gonna take it there."

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.200
"Well you didn't have to, believe me."

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.380
The second act exposes Chris to a bunch of white people all fascinated with Chris’s blackness.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.500
Party guests want to know what it’s like to be black in America, and even

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:14.870
feel compelled to tell him that "black is in fashion." There is a term for white fascination

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:19.489
with black culture that can help us better understand Get Out: negrophilia. The term

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:24.540
emerged in 1920’s Paris to describe the craze for black culture amongst the hipsters

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.120
of the day. Writer Petrine Archer-Straw writes that, for these Parisians, blackness was a

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.780
sign of young Parisian “modernity.”

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:34.900
"Such a privilege to be able to experience another person's culture, ya know what I'm sayin?"

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.280
But this had a problematic flipside:

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:43.180
“Black personalities were either lionised or demonised in a manner that denied normality.”

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:48.480
In other words, they could never be, you know, actual people, and instead existed as one of two

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:54.200
stereotypes: brilliant artists or uncultured
animals. Throughout the film, we see both

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.400
sides of this. Chris is celebrated for his cultural background, yet as the creepy brother

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.160
reminds us: "if you really pushed your body -

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.720
you'd be a f***ing beast."

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:09.680
To paraphrase Archer-Straw, in both cases, it’s the white perspective that decides

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:15.159
how black people are defined. And this negrophilia isn’t just confined to the desire for black

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.680
culture, it can be the desire for the black
body itself both sexually;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:25.780
"So, is it true? Is it better?"

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:26.740
and athletically.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.500
"So Chris, what's your sport?"

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:33.720
And while you might be tempted to think “is it so bad to be desired because you’re sexy and strong?”,

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:38.599
well the answer is, yeah. Negrophilia isn’t about truly understanding black people

00:04:38.599 --> 00:04:44.150
and their culture, it’s about using them to satisfy your own desires. This is not understanding

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:49.290
humans, it’s collecting them. And while Get Out exemplifies classic Negrophilia, we

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:54.420
see it take a leap forward to the literal colonization of the black body in the third act.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.720
Early in the film we learn about Grandpa Armitage’s experience of being bested by

00:04:58.720 --> 00:04:59.780
a black athlete.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:04.800
"My dad's claim to fame was beat by Jesse Owens in the qualifying round for

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.920
the Berlin Olympics in 1936. Those are the ones where-"

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:08.920
"Owens won in front of Hitler."

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.500
Yeah, what a moment, what a moment.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:16.620
Hitler's up there with all this perfect Aryan race bullshit and this black dude comes along

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.780
proves him wrong in front of the entire world. Amazing."

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:21.560
"Tough break for your dad though."

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.680
"Yeah... he almost got over it."

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:27.860
Well it turns out that his inability to get over it lead him to have

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.320
his brain implanted into an athletic black body.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.800
And Grandma Armitage? Well, it turns out her brain was placed in Georgina so she could

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:40.210
spend her golden years playing dress up as a beautiful black woman. And of course Chris

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.820
is set on track for a similar fate when he is auctioned off to a blind man who

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:49.130
wants his award winning photographer’s eyes. And in an extreme version of liberal racism,

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:53.820
Chris’ new owner refuses to attribute his purchase to racism: "Please don't lump me

00:05:53.820 --> 00:06:01.100
in with that, I don't give a s*** what color you are. What I want is deeper.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:05.160
I want those things you see through." While a white person wanting to literally

00:06:05.169 --> 00:06:10.039
transform into a black person might sound preposterous, there is at least one American

00:06:10.039 --> 00:06:14.450
who wishes this was a reality: "Well, I don't identify as African American, I identify as

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:18.550
black, so I'm part of the pan-African diaspora."

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:24.300
The film also offers insight into the psychological experience of black Americans navigating white America.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.280
Before the title screen, we see a black guy lost in white suburbs-

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.120
a metaphor for what’s to come:

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:37.120
"They got me out here in this creepy, confusing a** suburba.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:42.940
"Gosh, I'm serious though. I feel like a sore thumb out here."

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:43.800
The physical maze

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:48.540
of white suburbia sets a clear parallel for
the emotional maze that Chris will have to

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:53.800
navigate. Many of Chris’ interactions seem modulated specifically for his white audience,

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.880
like during interactions with the police,
Rose seems content to make a scene while the

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.140
only thing Chris wants to do is not piss off a cop.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.620
"No, no, no fuck that. You don't have to give him your I.D. because you haven't done anything wrong."

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:06.160
"Baby, baby. It's okay, c'mon."

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:07.520
Or when he suspects someone's

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:12.400
been tampering with his phone, he quickly drops the issue to not make a scene with Rose’s family.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:13.560
"Forget it, nevermind."

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.680
"No, no, no, stop. Don't do that. Don't-"

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.080
"Okay, okay. I'm sorry, it's all good. Right?"

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:22.360
Even something as simple as meeting his girlfriend’s parents leads Chris to worry:

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:25.410
"Do they know I'm... do they know I'm black?"

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:29.200
And being one of the only people of color at the party leaves Chris anxiously trying

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:32.060
to navigate this overwhelmingly white space.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:37.560
"All I know is sometimes, if there's too many white people I get nervous, you know?"

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.220
Philosopher Frantz Fanon described his own,

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:45.040
similar, experience as a black man living in 20th century France. Fanon describes meeting

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:49.870
“the white man’s eyes” as placing a
burdensome weight upon him, one that left

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:55.600
him feeling uncomfortable in his own body, and out of place in a world dominated by whiteness.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.080
Like Fanon, Chris is burdened by the weight of trying to fit in a world from which he’s

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:01.860
fundamentally excluded.

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:03.520
"Good to see another brother around here."

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:07.460
"Hi."

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.280
He's caught trying to integrate into Rose’s world, while at

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:12.860
the same time trying to retain his identity.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.160
"It's like they haven't met a black person that doesn't work for them."

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:18.000
This forces Chris to be two people at the same time:

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.660
a black man with his own identity, and, Rose’s black boyfriend who is nice to everyone

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.300
no matter how absurd their questions.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:38.050
To navigate this, Chris has to silence his own identity, a process made literal during

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:42.840
a late-night chat with Rose’s mother: eventually viewing his own reality through a screen.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.820
"Now you're in the Sunken Place."

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:49.260
This is the name for a hypnotic state in which one’s mind is separated from their body,

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:53.590
and they are left to passively view their own experiences through a screen. When asked

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:57.700
about the meaning of the Sunken Place, Jordan Peele tweeted that “The Sunken Place means

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:02.380
we’re marginalized. No matter how hard we scream, the system silences us.”

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:08.300
According to this logic, any society built upon inequality will inevitably leave those on the outside

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:13.200
without a voice, or at best, with a tempered version of their voice. One’s true identity

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.670
has the remain in the background for the sake of going with the flow. We can understand

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:22.580
the sunken place better with some help from sociologist W.E.B. Du Bois, who coined the

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:27.680
term double consciousness. According to Du Bois, double consciousness is the internal

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.011
conflict experienced by African Americans living in a structurally racist society. These

00:09:33.011 --> 00:09:37.110
societal conditions lead black people to see themselves through the perspective of the

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:42.500
dominant societal force, in this case, white people. Chris is repeatedly evaluated by the

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:48.200
measuring tape of white society, and in particular, certain forms of white desire. The experience

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:53.180
of being constantly examined leads to what Du Bois calls a “two-ness” in the black soul:

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:10.660
This imposition leads to confusion about the authenticity

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:12.060
of one’s experiences,

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:16.060
"She got in my head and I don't think that was fucked up shit that I don't want to think about."

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:19.180
and for Chris, this double consciousness has left him feeling

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:20.970
like a helpless spectator.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:25.120
If you were wondering why Chris seems to be haunted by the deer he and Rose killed it

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:30.280
may not because he’s some kind of PETA activist, but because watching helplessly as a living

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.480
thing died probably reminded him of his mother’s death.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:54.760
In other words, the sunken place seems

00:10:54.840 --> 00:11:00.950
not only be a hypnosis technique, but a metaphor for his own life as a helpless spectator.

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:06.500
This fear of losing oneself is made literal in the Coagula procedure, in which black consciousness

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:11.760
is literally pushed to the background to make room for the white mind; thus he risks being

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.710
permanently cast into the sunken place. Chris is able to avoid this fate by plugging his

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:21.110
ears with cotton picked from his chair which brings to mind poet Audre Lorde’s famous

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:25.340
insistence that “the master’s tools will never dismantle the master’s house.”

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:29.230
For Lorde this meant that it was futile for the oppressed to use the logic of the oppressor

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:33.570
in their attempts at resistance. Instead, Get Out suggests the opposite: Chris uses

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:39.440
a type of labor associated with slavery, picking cotton, to assert his own identity and avoid

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:44.480
a type of slavery. Chris not only uses cotton to avoid bodily colonization, he re-purposes

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:49.820
a bocce ball, arguably a symbol of the white elite, to take out Jeremy. Not to say that

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:54.310
bocce balls are blatantly white objects, but, the only thing whiter would be playing hacky

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:58.720
sack at a Fish concert. And, calling back to the symbol of Chris’ spectatorship,

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:02.900
Chris takes a mounted deer and goes straight for Dean's jugular with it, a sign

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:08.180
he’s overcome the powerlessness he once feared. Chris’ emancipation from this near-enslavement

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:11.810
can be seen as a radical assertion of his black identity.

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:16.220
While Chris is able to escape the Armitage plantation of horrors, the real hero of Get Out,

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:20.360
and the film’s comic relief, is his best friend, Rod. Throughout the film Rod

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:22.160
serves as the voice of reason.

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:24.100
"White people love making people sex slaves and shit."

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:29.240
And eventually goes to the police once he puts together a theory for why Chris has gone missing.

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:31.960
After a, um, revealing conversation with Rose

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:34.200
"I know you think about fucking me, Rod."

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.900
he decides it’s time to come to Chris’ rescue.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:40.580
"Lyin' b*tch. She is lying like a mother f*cker. I know that."

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:46.260
But before Rod saves the day, the film’s
most terrifying scene comes from an unexpected source.

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:49.860
We all know what is likely to happen if the police show up and see a black man

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:53.580
kneeling over the bleeding body of a white woman. While the film’s intended ending

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:56.080
was this devastating worst-case scenario.

00:12:56.080 --> 00:13:00.340
Peele opted for a more triumphant conclusion in the final cut. But the moment between the

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:05.020
police sirens and Rod getting out of the car is a stark reminder that the real monster

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:10.220
of Get Out isn’t a fictional boogeyman,
but rather, the horrors of the system itself,

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:14.140
something that cannot be heroically defeated at the conclusion of a film. And the other

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:16.480
lesson of Get Out? Well that one is more obvious:

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:28.520
"I'm T.S. mother f*ckin' A. We handle shit. That's what we do. Consider this situation f*ckin handled."

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:30.100
Thanks for watching, guys. Peace!

